  },
  {
   "angle": 0,
   "groupIds": [],
   "frameId": null,
   "roughness": 1,
   "opacity": 100,
   "strokeWidth": 2,
   "strokeStyle": "solid",
   "fillStyle": "solid",
   "seed": 1353533343,
   "version": 1,
   "versionNonce": 1708413825,
   "isDeleted": false,
   "boundElements": null,
   "updated": 1,
   "link": null,
   "locked": false,
   "type": "text",
   "x": 70,
   "y": 4800,
   "width": 854.2800000000001,
   "height": 105.0,
   "text": "1.  Why does a window function avoid a self-join? What would the self-join version of 'top 3 per user' look like?\n2.  cache() returned 0.8x here. Describe a query in THIS dataset where it would genuinely win, and say why.\n3.  Lab 3 reads Silver, not Bronze or Gold. Why is Silver the right input for an analytics layer?\n4.  A broadcast join on a 2 GB dimension table - what exactly goes wrong, and on which machine?\n5.  The incremental filter uses event_timestamp. What breaks if events arrive LATE, after the watermark moved?\n6.  SCD-2 keeps every version. What does that cost you, and when is a plain overwrite the better call?",
   "originalText": "1.  Why does a window function avoid a self-join? What would the self-join version of 'top 3 per user' look like?\n2.  cache() returned 0.8x here. Describe a query in THIS dataset where it would genuinely win, and say why.\n3.  Lab 3 reads Silver, not Bronze or Gold. Why is Silver the right input for an analytics layer?\n4.  A broadcast join on a 2 GB dimension table - what exactly goes wrong, and on which machine?\n5.  The incremental filter uses event_timestamp. What breaks if events arrive LATE, after the watermark moved?\n6.  SCD-2 keeps every version. What does that cost you, and when is a plain overwrite the better call?",
   "fontSize": 14,
   "fontFamily": 1,
